Let $\mathbb{D}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|<1\}$ denote the open unit disk and $\mathbb{T}=\{z\in\mathbb{C}:|z|=1\}$ the unit circle. Let $S$ denote the class of functions $f$ that are analytic and univalent in $\mathbb{D}$ with $f(0)=0$ and $f'(0)=1$. Suppose $f(z)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n z^n$ is analytic in $\mathbb{D}$ and that the Taylor coefficients satisfy $\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}|a_n|<+\infty$ (so that $f$ extends continuously to the closed unit disk and the image $f(\mathbb{T})$ is a continuous closed curve in $\mathbb{C}$). Determine whether it is possible for $f(\mathbb{T})$ to have positive two-dimensional Lebesgue measure under either of the following hypotheses:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $f\in S$;
\item[(b)] more generally, $f'(z)\neq 0$ for every $z\in\mathbb{D}$ (i.e.\ $f$ is locally univalent in $\mathbb{D}$).
\end{enumerate}

